WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:13.480
The story begins in 1812 in France
and a young boy called Louis Braille.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.520
He lost his sight in one eye
when he was three.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:22.040
This was the culprit,
a sharp tool called an awl

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:25.040
which he was playing around with
in his father's workshop.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:29.480
His eye got infected and the
infection spread to the other eye.

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:34.040
By five, Louis Braille
was completely blind.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:41.600
But he was determined and he went on
to win a scholarship at France's

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:43.880
Royal Institute for Blind Youth.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.800
At the time the system
for reading was pretty basic,

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:52.320
just raised letters on a page.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:56.040
So he started working on a
new code that would be quicker

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:57.120
and more efficient.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:03.240
By the time he was 15, Braille's
new system was basically complete.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:06.960
And the first edition
was published in 1829.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:12.880
Braille's system was based on a
military code called "night writing."

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:17.480
Invented by captain Charles Barbier,
it was a system of embossed dots

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:21.040
and dashes that soldiers could
read safely with their fingers

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:22.720
on the battlefield at night.

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:25.520
Braille simplified the system,

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:29.440
reducing the cells from
12 to 6 raised dots,

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.600
so that they were the ideal size for
a fingertip to feel with one touch.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:40.000
Braille created his raised dots
using an awl, the same sharp tool

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.240
that had caused his blindness and a
flat grill to keep the lines straight

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:45.280
and readable.

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:49.800
It's read from left to right
like other European scripts.

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:53.000
Braille is not a language,
it's a system of writing,

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:56.160
which means it can be adapted
to different languages.

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:00.800
Braille codes have been developed
for maths and scientific formulae.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:04.480
Braille loved music and
invented a system of Braille

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:06.520
for writing music too.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:12.720
But the medical establishment was
conservative and Braille's innovation

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:14.400
was slow to be adopted.

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:20.120
He died of tuberculosis aged 43,
two years before his system was

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:23.200
finally taught at the institute
where he'd been a student.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.560
100 years later, he was reburied
at the Panthéon in Paris

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:30.120
in honour of his work.

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:34.960
Though his local village insisted
on keeping his hands.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:39.280
Over time, the system did spread
throughout the French speaking world.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:44.000
By 1882 it was in use across Europe
and had reached North America

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:45.640
by 1916.

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:51.840
A universal Braille code for English
was formalised in 1932.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:57.160
Braille has been revolutionary for
many blind people around the world.

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:59.240
But with the rise
of new technologies,

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:04.040
including computers that talk,
literacy rates are on the decline.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:09.720
In 1999, a second eponym was given
to this extraordinary man.

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:16.440
A rare type of asteroid was named
9969 Braille by Nasa.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:20.320
A timeless tribute,
to a great human being.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:25.200
